S1_1 Taxable refunds -- only include the Minnesota tax refund if we claimed it
as an itemized tax cost in 2023 (for 2022). Which we did; see 2022 A5a

csvtk is pretty awesome:

- csvtk grep -f Expense -r -p 'Medical' deductions.csv | csvtk summary -f Amount:sum
- csvtk pretty deductions.csv

- Edit the `opentaxsolver.txt` to add comments to the capital gains stuff; it'll
  be included in the PDF.
- PDF forms can be filled out with `evince` (or `mupdf-gl`). Signatures can be
  added with `xournalpp` using `~/Pictures/Signature.png` and
  `~/Pictures/Signature_BBB.png`.
- For taxes, ledger only needs the bank statements (Bank & CC). Pull
  them into a separate `everything.dat` and run the report on that.
- **Download from the bank in CSV**. Don't bother with the other formats; ledger
  can't read anything but CSV. All you're looking for is the deductions for
  itemization, anyway, and that's going to be medical and donations from the
  accounts; you don't need the entire, big-picture `everything.dat`.

  - You'll still need to fuss with the payees and everything; convert, dump, re-
    categorize, re-import. This will required data files and the executables in
    `~/Documents/Finance/ledger`
  - `ledger convert CC2022.tmp --inBBB-date-format %m/%d/%Y -f CC2022.dat --account Liabilityy:CC --rich-data`
  - `ledger -f taxes2022.dat payees --period 2022 Expenses:Unknown | sort | uniq >> payees.csv`
  - `ledger -f taxes2022.dat csv --csv-format "%(id),%D,%A,%t\n" tag deductable | ./bin/pivot | ./bin/sum | sort > taxes2022.csv`
  - `ledger -f everything.dat register show deductable`

- Feds need:

  - W-2
  - All 1099s (investments, IRAs, etc)

- Minnesota requires the the AMT worksheet, whether it was necessary for federal
  or not, so just fill it out
- If any stock trades, fill in D, otherwise, capital gains can be summarized

: Schedule D

  > If distributions, line 13, are your only investment items to report, you don’t
  > have to fill out Schedule D; they go directly on your 1040 or 1040A return.
  >
  > -- <https://www.bankrate.com/investing/schedule-d-reporting-your-capital-gains-or-losses/>

: Documents to include:

  > When you file a tax return electronically, an electronic copy of your W-2 and
  > other income forms, such as 1099s, is created. These electronic versions of
  > your income forms are transmitted with your return to the IRS. Since alternate
  > versions of your income forms are not created when you send a paper return,
  > you must attach copies of your physical W-2s and 1099s to the front page of
  > your 1040 form. Although you may receive multiple forms at the end of the year
  > to help prepare your return, you only need to attach forms that show types of
  > income you received. It is not necessary to attach copies of other forms that
  > show payments you made, such as 1098 forms you receive for mortgage or student
  > loan interest you paid.
  >
  > The IRS does not require taxpayers to attach receipts or proof of expense
  > payments claimed on tax returns, but you must hold onto receipts and copies of
  > any other items used to prepare your return, and keep them handy. In the event
  > a return you file is selected for an audit, you’ll need to show proof of your
  > expenses to your examiner, or items you claimed could be disallowed.
  >
  > -- <https://pocketsense.com/documents-should-attached-tax-return-paper-filing-8209.html>

: AMT

  > *Who Must File*
  >
  > Attach Form 6251 to your return if any of the following statements are true.
  >
  > - Form 6251, line 7, is greater than line 10.
  > - You claim any general business credit, and either line 6 (in Part I) of Form
  > - 3800 or line 25 of Form 3800 is more than zero.
  > - You claim the qualified electric vehicle credit (Form 8834), the personal
  >   use part of the alternative fuel vehicle refueling property credit (Form
  >   8911), or the credit for prior year minimum tax (Form 8801).
  > - The total of Form 6251, lines 2c through 3, is negative and line 7 would be
  >   greater than line 10 if you didn’t take into account lines 2c through 3.
  >
  > -- <https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i6251>

## FILED

- `fed_combined.pdf` - the US submission: Income statements and (non-instruction) US forms
- `mn_combined.pdf` - the MN submission: the (non-instruction) MN forms
- `mn_forms/` - all of the Minnesota forms, filled out
- `federal_forms/` - all of the US forms, filled out

The `*_combined.pdf` were created with `cpdf`, selecting the non-instruction pages:

- *US*: `cpdf W-2_Form_2022_LName-Lname2.pdf 1 opentaxsolver_out_signed.pdf worksheet_done.pdf Lname2_1099_5418_22.pdf 3 2022-Individual-TOD-2745-Consolidated-Form-1099.pdf 1 2022-Individual-0365-Consolidated-Form-1099.pdf 1 -o combined.pdf`
- *MN*: `cpdf m1_22_signed.pdf m1c_22.pdf 1 m1m_22.pdf 1-2 m1mt_22.pdf 1 m1ref_22.pdf 1 m1sa_22.pdf 1 m1w_22.pdf -o combined.pdf`

Minnesota can't be completed until the Federal is.

The MN submission *includes* the entire federal submission.

Minnesota requires filling out 6251 (AMT) whether or not the feds require it. So just do it.

The Minnesota submission requires:

- The MN forms
- `f6251.pdf`, the AMT worksheet
- The entire federal submission

